- How to handle concurrent edits in a collaborative editor? 
- Can you quantify memory overhead and compare with a pure block-based rope?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a scalable data structure for tracking peak occupancy across a large domain with many overlapping intervals—a common problem in campaign management, resource allocation, and scheduling systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Implicit segment tree**: Enables efficient operations over a massive coordinate space (up to 2^31-1) without building a full tree
- **Range add, range max with argmax**: Combines lazy propagation for updates with maintaining both value and position of the maximum
- **Incremental updates**: Insert and erase operations dynamically modify the tree state
- **Handling duplicates**: Multiple intervals covering the same coordinate naturally accumulate via additive weights

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor() {
    this.max = 0;
    this.argMax = 0;
    this.lazy = 0;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
  }
}

function rangeAdd(node, L, R, ql, qr, delta) {
  if (qr < L || R < ql) return;
  if (ql <= L && R <= qr) {
    node.max += delta;
    node.lazy += delta;
    return;
  }
  push(node);
  const mid = (L + R) >>> 1;
  rangeAdd(node.left, L, mid, ql, qr, delta);
  rangeAdd(node.right, mid + 1, R, ql, qr, delta);
  pull(node);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle coordinate compression for non-uniform distributions?
- What are the time complexities for insert, erase, and query operations?
- How would you extend this to support minimum queries or range sum queries?

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time**: O(log N) per insertInterval, eraseInterval, and queryGlobalMax, where N is the coordinate domain size (2^31)
- **Space**: O(k log N) where k is the number of active intervals, since only nodes touched by operations are created (sparse representation)

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a mutable string buffer with fast substring comparisons, a common editor feature.

## Key Concepts
- Implicit treap (rope) for dynamic strings
- Subtree size and rolling hash maintenance
- Split/merge operations and rehashing

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node {
  constructor(block){
    this.block = block;
    this.left = null;
    this.right = null;
    this.size = block.length;
    this.hash = hashBlock(block);
  }
}
function update(n){
  if(!n) return;
  n.size = (n.left?.size ?? 0) + (n.right?.size ?? 0) + n.block.length;
  n.hash = combineHash(n.left?.hash, hashBlock(n.block), n.right?.hash);
}
```

